{
  "image_name": "0a6054921421d0f9f74d41642371567a.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how bacterial transcription and translation are reorganized as cells move from rapid growth to nutrient limitation and finally growth arrest. In the exponential phase, RNA polymerase is concentrated at rRNA and housekeeping promoters, supported by growth-associated factors, and many ribosomes are actively translating, reflecting strong production of ribosomal components and proteins.\n\nAs cells enter early stationary phase, the alarmone pathway involving RelA and (p)ppGpp, together with DksA, appears to suppress rRNA transcription and redirect RNA polymerase away from growth programs. At the same time, alternative regulation linked to RpoS and stress-response promoters becomes more prominent. Ribosomes begin to change state, including formation of inactive 100S dimers and association with ribosome-protective factors, while some translational machinery remains active on selected transcripts.\n\nIn growth arrest, the shift is more complete: growth-related transcription factors are crossed out, stress-associated transcription predominates, and ribosomes are largely protected, inactivated, or remodeled rather than broadly engaged in protein synthesis. The image also suggests selective control of mRNA translation and decay, with some transcripts preserved and others degraded. Overall, the figure conveys a coordinated transition from biosynthetic growth to stress survival through global changes in promoter usage, sigma-factor control, and ribosome activity.",
